- Initial limit: A higher initial credit limit may indicate better creditworthiness or bank trust, positively correlated with high credit scoring.  
- BIRTHDATE: Age can affect credit scoring; typically, middle-aged clients may have better credit scores due to stable income and credit history.  
- SEX: Gender itself is unlikely to have a direct causal effect but might show some statistical bias in data.  
- EDU: Higher education levels (University, College) often correlate with better income and credit management, thus higher credit scoring.  
- INCOME: Higher income generally leads to better credit scoring due to greater repayment capacity.  
- TERM: Longer credit terms might increase risk, possibly lowering credit scoring.  
- Credit history rating: Better ratings (A1, A2, A3) indicate good credit history and higher credit scoring; worse ratings (D, E categories) indicate low credit scoring.  
- LV_AREA: Some regions may have different economic conditions affecting credit scoring.  
- LV_SETTLEMENTNAME: City or settlement may reflect economic status, affecting credit scoring.  
- INDUSTRYNAME: Employment in stable or high-income industries (Finance, IT, Medicine) may correlate with better credit scoring.  
- PDN: Higher probability of default (PDN) directly correlates with lower credit scoring.  
- UNDERAGECHILDRENCOUNT: More dependents may strain finances, possibly lowering credit scoring.  
- FAMILYSTATUS: Married or stable family status may correlate with better financial stability and credit scoring.  

